Below is a **single‑file C# console application** that shows the most common ways you can use lambda expressions.  
The program is self‑contained, compiles with any recent version of the .NET SDK, and prints out the results of each example so you can see the lambdas in action.

```csharp
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// Lambda Expressions Demo
//
// Build & Run:
//   dotnet run
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Linq.Expressions;   // for Expression<TDelegate>

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // 1️⃣  Lambda as a Func<TInput, TResult>
        Func<int, int> square = x => x * x;     // one‑parameter lambda
        Console.WriteLine($"square(5) = {square(5)}");   // 25

        // 2️⃣  Lambda as an Action<T>
        Action<string> greet = name => Console.WriteLine($"Hello, {name}!");
        greet("Alice");   // prints: Hello, Alice!

        // 3️⃣  Lambda in a LINQ query
        List<int> numbers = new List<int> { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };

        //    a) Filter even numbers
        var evens = numbers.Where(n => n % 2 == 0);
        Console.WriteLine("Even numbers: " + string.Join(", ", evens));   // 2, 4

        //    b) Project each number to its square
        var squares = numbers.Select(n => n * n);
        Console.WriteLine("Squares: " + string.Join(", ", squares));     // 1, 4, 9, 16, 25

        // 4️⃣  Lambda that captures an outer variable
        int multiplier = 3;
        Func<int, int> multiplyByMultiplier = x => x * multiplier;
        Console.WriteLine($"multiplyByMultiplier(4) = {multiplyByMultiplier(4)}"); // 12

        // 5️⃣  Lambda as an event handler
        // (Only demonstrates syntax; no UI framework here.)
        // For example, in WinForms or WPF you could write:
        // button.Click += (sender, e) => Console.WriteLine("Button clicked!");

        // 6️⃣  Lambda as an expression tree (used by LINQ providers)
        Expression<Func<int, int>> expr = x => x * x;
        Console.WriteLine($"Expression body: {expr.Body}");   // x * x

        // The Expression can be inspected or compiled:
        var compiled = expr.Compile();
        Console.WriteLine($"compiled(6) = {compiled(6)}");   // 36

        // 7️⃣  Multiple parameters
        Func<int, int, int> add = (a, b) => a + b;
        Console.WriteLine($"add(7, 8) = {add(7, 8)}");   // 15

        // 8️⃣  Lambda with no parameters
        Func<string> greetMe = () => "Greetings from a parameterless lambda!";
        Console.WriteLine(greetMe());   // prints the message

        // 9️⃣  Lambda that returns a collection
        Func<IEnumerable<int>, IEnumerable<int>> filterGreaterThan = seq =>
        {
            int threshold = 3;
            return seq.Where(n => n > threshold);
        };
        Console.WriteLine("Filter > 3: " + string.Join(", ", filterGreaterThan(numbers)));
    }
}
```

### What you’ll see when you run it

```
square(5) = 25
Hello, Alice!
Even numbers: 2, 4
Squares: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25
multiplyByMultiplier(4) = 12
Expression body: x * x
compiled(6) = 36
add(7, 8) = 15
Greetings from a parameterless lambda!
Filter > 3: 4, 5
```

### Highlights

| Example | What it shows |
|---------|---------------|
| `Func<int, int> square = x => x * x;` | Lambda assigned to a delegate type (`Func<>`). |
| `Action<string> greet = name => …;` | Lambda assigned to an `Action<>`. |
| `numbers.Where(n => n % 2 == 0)` | Lambda as the predicate for a LINQ method. |
| `x => x * multiplier` | Capturing a variable from the surrounding scope. |
| `Expression<Func<int, int>> expr = x => x * x;` | Lambda as an expression tree (used by EF, LINQ‑to‑SQL, etc.). |
| `() => "…"` | Lambda with no parameters. |
| Multiple parameters (`(a, b) => a + b`). | |
| `Func<IEnumerable<int>, IEnumerable<int>>` with a block body (`{ … }`). | Lambda with a block body that can contain multiple statements. |

Feel free to copy this code into a new console app (`dotnet new console`) and experiment—add your own lambdas, chain LINQ methods, or hook one up to a UI event in a WPF/WinForms project. Happy coding!
